any structure step ذ that is ת during that scene.
Some scenes will have these structure tags, but many will not.
KEY POINT: Be prepared to change your scene ´ when you start writing individual
scenes.
When it comes to actually writing a scene, you may find that the basic action らring in that scene
is not what you thought. You will only know that for sure when you get "inside" the scene and write it. So
be flexible. What's important at this point in the process is to get an overview of what you think the single
main action of each scene will be.
Be aware that the average █ movie has 传 to ŷ scenes. A з ordinarily has twice
that number and, depending on length and genre, possibly a great many more.
Your story may have subplots, or subsections, that when woven ě create the plot. If you have
more than one subplot or subsection, label each scene with a Г and subsection number. This will
allow you to look at the scenes of each subplot as a separate unit and make sure each subplot ف
