Once you have the complete scene ´ before you, see if you need to make the following
changes:
■ Reorder scenes. First, focus on getting the overall sequence of the story right. Then look at the
Д between individual scenes.
■ Combine scenes. Writers often create a new scene for no other reason than to get in a good line of
¢. Whenever possible, combine scenes so that each one is packed, but make sure each
scene 但 essentially one action.
■ Cut or add scenes. Always trim fat. Remember, story pacing has to do not only with the length of a
scene but also with the choice of scenes. Once you have trimmed all the fat, you may find € in
the scene ´ that require a whole new scene. If so, add it to the list in the right spot.
KEY POINT: Order the scenes by structure, not ₂.
Most writers choose the next scene according to which action (scene) comes next in time. The result
is a padded story with many م scenes. Instead, you want to choose a scene by how it ‰s the
development of the hero. If it doesn't ‰ that development or set it up in a ş way, cut the scene.
